1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Anchoring opposite coasts of America

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:06,800
are three of the world's most visited and beloved parks.

3
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:10,140
From the forlorn stretch of beach that's now San Francisco's

4
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:12,740
green and glorious Golden Gate Park.

5
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:16,760
This is a landscape that was changed almost 98%.

6
00:00:16,860 --> 00:00:19,660
To Griffith Park, given to the city of angels

7
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:22,060
by a man who is anything but.

8
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:24,960
People recognize that he's a leader,

9
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:28,700
but at the same time, they kind of roll their eyes.

10
00:00:28,701 --> 00:00:31,940
To Manhattan's Central Park, grown from hundreds

11
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:33,440
of rocky acres.

12
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:38,940
Central Park is really America's first urban park.

13
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:42,640
Next, City Parks on Modern Marvels.

14
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:03,700
What could be an oil painting by the father of Impressionism,

15
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:08,280
Claude Monet, is but a blink away from joggers training

16
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:10,120
for a marathon.

17
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:12,940
Around each corner, hikers discover something new

18
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:15,420
to delight the eye.

19
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:20,860
And skaters ballet on ice in the shadow of skyscrapers.

20
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:25,200
Today's city park is a living organism embraced

21
00:01:25,201 --> 00:01:27,940
Today's city park is a living organism,

22
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,500
embracing both Victorian sensibilities

23
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:35,940
and the desires of this century's active population.

24
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:38,940
It's a magical place whose visitors, rich and poor,

25
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:41,960
share in a democratic dream first realized

26
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:45,360
over 150 years ago.

27
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:48,860
Born of pure necessity, the urban park was the only escape

28
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:52,220
from the clutter, clamor, and filth of the industrialized

29
00:01:52,221 --> 00:01:56,520
America's earliest city parks faced nearly insurmountable

30
00:01:56,620 --> 00:01:59,620
obstacles, including difficult natural terrain,

31
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:02,360
as well as lack of modern machinery.

32
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:06,560
San Francisco's Golden Gate Park grew out of the sand.

33
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:11,240
Los Angeles's Griffith Park was the brainchild of a madman.

34
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:14,780
And out of the middle of Manhattan, once a rocky wasteland,

35
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:18,240
grew an oasis known as Central Park.

36
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:26,020
The earliest open spaces available to public use

37
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:29,560
were in 3rd century BC Athens, Greece.

38
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:32,460
The Greek gardens, both public and private,

39
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:34,860
were used by philosophers such as Plato

40
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:37,660
for the purpose of meeting with students.

41
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:41,080
The Romans, who greatly admired and copied the Greeks,

42
00:02:41,180 --> 00:02:43,400
also created public parks and plazas,

43
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:47,640
erecting monuments to honor emperors, generals, and gods.

44
00:02:47,740 --> 00:02:49,240
It was a very popular place to be

45
00:02:49,241 --> 00:02:51,740
emperors, generals, and gods.

46
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:53,940
It was their way of inspiring the masses

47
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:57,380
to appreciate the power and glory of the Roman Empire

48
00:02:57,480 --> 00:02:58,380
and way of life.

49
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:05,460
By the 1500s, the English park was a highly exclusive enclosure

50
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:09,720
to be enjoyed only by royalty and the nobility.

51
00:03:09,820 --> 00:03:13,940
The word park actually means a hunting park.

52
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:18,940
Royal hunting parks go way, way back in antiquity.

53
00:03:18,941 --> 00:03:29,920
Also public spaces, places in ancient cities where people could meet, such as the Greek Agora or the Roman Forum.

54
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:32,600
These were public spaces.

55
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:43,320
In Europe, you had the royal park where the king's hunting grounds and everybody else went in there at peril of their life of being cut.

56
00:03:43,321 --> 00:03:46,520
But by 1592, Parliament passed an act

57
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:50,120
forbidding the wealthy to enclose land for their own needs

58
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:53,360
within three miles of the gates of the city of London.

59
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:55,760
This was in hopes of preserving some land

60
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:59,340
for ordinary citizens' public recreation.

61
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:03,480
In 1649, Parliament presented to London's citizenry

62
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:07,240
Richmond Park, formerly held by King Charles.

63
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:12,780
In London, you had Hyde Park, you had St. James Park,

64
00:04:12,781 --> 00:04:17,960
Park, Green Park, Kensington Gardens, Richmond Park,

65
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:20,860
all properties of the crown.

66
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:27,400
But the creation of parks with trees and lakes

67
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:33,400
and picturesque scenery, this is really a 19th century movement

68
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:37,740
that started in England.

69
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:41,500
But quickly moved to America because the US, as well as

70
00:04:41,501 --> 00:04:45,000
was in the midst of the rapidly growing industrial age,

71
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:48,140
and this developing country's need for open space

72
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:51,240
could no longer be ignored.

73
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:55,140
We're talking about the age of industrialization.

74
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:59,160
This is the time when all of a sudden cities

75
00:04:59,260 --> 00:05:02,560
are going to get much larger.

76
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:06,200
People realize that the rural village, the village

77
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:10,500
with its village green, another kind of public space,

78
00:05:10,501 --> 00:05:17,500
is America's outgrowing that shape and that size.

79
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:20,940
It was unimaginable to America's first settlers

80
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:24,340
that their wild North American continent would someday soon

81
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:26,440
need to be conserved.

82
00:05:26,540 --> 00:05:28,660
The fathers of our nation's oldest cities

83
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:31,960
believe the common areas or small green squares built

84
00:05:32,060 --> 00:05:36,760
into their city's design would suffice for their recreation.

85
00:05:36,761 --> 00:05:40,460
But by the 1850s, urbanization and industrialization

86
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:43,500
robbed citizens of that open space.

87
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,000
And overcrowding was the least of it.

88
00:05:46,100 --> 00:05:48,740
In many cities, but particularly in old New York,

89
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:51,680
cattle and sheep were herded through the dirt streets

90
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:54,420
with no regard to sanitation.

91
00:05:54,520 --> 00:05:56,580
Garbage was thrown into the gutters.

92
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:58,440
Water was contaminated.

93
00:05:58,540 --> 00:06:01,480
And hundreds of citizens, but particularly the poor who

94
00:06:01,580 --> 00:06:05,380
had no means of escape, died from malaria, typhoid, cholera,

95
00:06:05,381 --> 00:06:06,920
and smallpox.

96
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:09,860
Once the streets were paved over, the clatter from horse

97
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:13,320
hooves and cartwheels was deafening.

98
00:06:13,420 --> 00:06:15,620
With no parks yet available, little wonder

99
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:18,900
the masses fled to the only clean patch of green grass

100
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,400
and blue sky they had, rural cemeteries, which were often

101
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:25,060
beautifully landscaped.

102
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:29,000
Here in America, our movement started in Boston

103
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:32,180
with Mount Auburn Cemetery.

104
00:06:32,181 --> 00:06:35,880
and quickly New York would not fall behind.

105
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:39,120
They created the beautiful Greenwood Cemetery,

106
00:06:39,121 --> 00:06:43,940
and because it was created as a landscape space,

107
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:47,560
there were rolling acres, and the monuments to the dead

108
00:06:47,561 --> 00:06:50,660
were conceived as works of art.

109
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:57,160
So naturally, people would go to walk beside the still waters

110
00:06:57,161 --> 00:07:01,480
and the green pastures among the monuments

111
00:07:01,481 --> 00:07:05,720
to commune with the shades of the dead.

112
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:07,780
But the rural cemetery couldn't hope

113
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:11,920
to accommodate both the dead and the living forever.

114
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:14,720
With our great waves of immigration

115
00:07:14,820 --> 00:07:17,020
into this city, into New York, there

116
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:22,700
were many people who thought, we must relieve the congestion

117
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:24,140
of the Lower East Side.

118
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:25,680
We must have a park.

119
00:07:25,780 --> 00:07:30,780
It was always meant to be a democratic space.

120
00:07:30,781 --> 00:07:34,520
New York's population tripled by 1850.

121
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:38,080
Tenements were going up, limiting light and ventilation.

122
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:41,900
The air was foul, sanitation almost nonexistent.

123
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,460
New York's population of 600,000

124
00:07:44,560 --> 00:07:47,200
had scarcely 100 acres of parkland.

125
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:50,140
William Cullen Bryant, editor of the New York Post,

126
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:53,200
was inspired by a recent trip to London's Hyde Park

127
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:55,240
in Kensington Gardens.

128
00:07:55,241 --> 00:08:00,780
He editorialized in favor of public parks.

129
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:07,180
A man named Andrew Jackson Downing had a magazine called The Horticulturist.

130
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:10,180
He was perhaps the most influential voice in America,

131
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:14,800
along with Mr. Bryant, in the campaign for public parks.

132
00:08:15,460 --> 00:08:18,160
The wealthy people wanted to come and ride their carriages,

133
00:08:18,640 --> 00:08:22,780
and they wanted the poor people and the immigrants who were flooding into the city

134
00:08:22,781 --> 00:08:28,780
to come and watch these people, to learn how to behave

135
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:32,780
and to give them the goals and aspirations of middle

136
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:35,520
and upper class America.

137
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:38,220
This was the birth of the urban park movement

138
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:41,640
in America, with Manhattan's Central Park leading the way.

139
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:47,980
William Cullen Bryant was inspired

140
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:50,740
by the writings of Walden author and transcendentalist,

141
00:08:50,741 --> 00:08:54,640
Henry David Thoreau, who spent two years communing with himself

142
00:08:54,740 --> 00:08:57,780
and nature in a log cabin on Walden Pond.

143
00:08:57,880 --> 00:09:00,480
City Parks will continue on Modern Marvels.

144
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:07,960
We now return to City Parks on Modern Marvels.

145
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:13,360
In 1856, the New York legislature

146
00:09:13,460 --> 00:09:17,660
approved America's first public park.

147
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:19,300
The area that became Central Park

148
00:09:19,301 --> 00:09:22,100
was actually the fringes of the city,

149
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:26,280
like many areas of cities where poor people live

150
00:09:26,380 --> 00:09:28,680
and improvements haven't been made yet.

151
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:30,180
Roads haven't been built.

152
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:32,880
Sewers haven't been established.

153
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:35,720
Central Park bordered 5th and 8th avenues

154
00:09:35,820 --> 00:09:39,620
to the east and west, 59th to 106th streets

155
00:09:39,720 --> 00:09:41,380
to the south and north.

156
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:44,960
But in the early 1850s, Manhattan was only settled

157
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:47,620
up to 42nd Street.

158
00:09:47,621 --> 00:09:55,960
They were very, very foresighted in taking 843 acres of land

159
00:09:56,060 --> 00:09:58,300
and envisioning that one day there

160
00:09:58,400 --> 00:10:00,100
would be a great metropolis around it,

161
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:06,100
because it was then still on the northern edge of the city.

162
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:10,840
The site for Central Park was a rocky, swampy, squalid mess.

163
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:12,880
Well-known writer and experimental farmer,

164
00:10:12,980 --> 00:10:17,620
Frederick Law Olmsted, was charged with removing residents.

165
00:10:17,621 --> 00:10:20,120
The city took the land for public domain,

166
00:10:20,220 --> 00:10:24,020
and those people were actually bought out.

167
00:10:24,120 --> 00:10:27,460
As Park superintendent, 35-year-old Olmsted

168
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:30,540
cleared the land with an unskilled workforce of Irish

169
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:32,700
and German immigrants.

170
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:36,140
These 3,800 laborers with horse-drawn carts

171
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:38,180
drained the swamps and marshes.

172
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:40,440
With thousands of pounds of gunpowder,

173
00:10:40,540 --> 00:10:45,120
they blasted out rock that lay inches below the surface.

174
00:10:45,121 --> 00:10:48,660
5 million cubic yards of stone, earth, and topsoil

175
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:51,920
were hand carted into or out of the park,

176
00:10:52,020 --> 00:10:57,760
turning over the entire park to a depth of 4 feet.

177
00:10:57,860 --> 00:11:01,500
In 1857, while the clearing operation took place,

178
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:04,700
a young English architect named Calvert Vaux

179
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:07,580
approached Frederick Law Olmsted.

180
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:11,240
Vaux encouraged Olmsted to join in a design competition

181
00:11:11,340 --> 00:11:13,340
for Central Park.

182
00:11:13,341 --> 00:11:18,320
Young Mr. Vox wanted to enter the design competition,

183
00:11:18,321 --> 00:11:24,160
but he wanted to do it with somebody that he felt would have weight

184
00:11:24,161 --> 00:11:26,960
and respect within the community,

185
00:11:26,961 --> 00:11:32,260
and he knew that Frederick Law Olmsted was his man.

186
00:11:33,060 --> 00:11:36,100
In my opinion, no surprise that these two won the prize,

187
00:11:36,101 --> 00:11:40,760
even though they are the only ones who did suggest and create a masterpiece.

188
00:11:40,761 --> 00:11:44,060
They named their winning entry Greensward.

189
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:48,000
Its naturalistic theme featured a landscape of meadows,

190
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:50,700
woodlands, and seven bodies of water,

191
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:53,400
all entirely man-made.

192
00:11:53,500 --> 00:11:55,800
Where you see beautiful lakes today,

193
00:11:55,900 --> 00:11:59,220
these were boggy, swampy areas.

194
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:04,120
Where you see the beautiful rock outcrops, yes, that was there,

195
00:12:04,220 --> 00:12:06,860
those wonderful bones of Central Park,

196
00:12:06,861 --> 00:12:10,560
The Manhattan schist bedrock outcrops.

197
00:12:10,660 --> 00:12:14,460
But there were no rolling meadows, and there were no trees.

198
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:18,100
The landscape was rocky.

199
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:20,400
The soil was thin.

200
00:12:20,500 --> 00:12:23,100
The vegetation was sparse.

201
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:26,820
Vaux's gift to the park were the pavilions, boathouses,

202
00:12:26,920 --> 00:12:28,520
bridges, and arches.

203
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:31,520
Architecturally, each bridge was unique.

204
00:12:36,860 --> 00:12:38,860
Vox was the professional.

205
00:12:38,960 --> 00:12:41,900
Vox was the architect.

206
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:43,700
Vox had drafting skills.

207
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:45,700
Vox could do renderings.

208
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:51,440
But Olmstead had the intellectual capacity

209
00:12:51,540 --> 00:13:00,420
to see the park as part of the civilization of young America.

210
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:03,120
Olmstead handled the landscaping.

211
00:13:03,220 --> 00:13:06,160
To create the lake located at the park center,

212
00:13:06,161 --> 00:13:09,000
He installed 95 miles of drainage pipes

213
00:13:09,100 --> 00:13:11,460
to carry off excess surface water

214
00:13:11,560 --> 00:13:13,800
and to allow for infusions of fresh water

215
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:16,640
from the Central Park's Croton Reservoir.

216
00:13:16,740 --> 00:13:19,940
In the winter of 1858, the lake was purposely flooded

217
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:24,680
so it would freeze over, and New Yorkers skated on natural ice

218
00:13:24,780 --> 00:13:26,140
for the very first time.

219
00:13:29,860 --> 00:13:32,520
Back then, skaters would look to Belvedere Castle

220
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:35,320
to learn if the ice was ready for skating.

221
00:13:35,321 --> 00:13:40,160
They would hoist a red ball over the, what is now,

222
00:13:40,260 --> 00:13:43,100
the Belvedere, the little castle in the park.

223
00:13:43,200 --> 00:13:45,660
And people could see that from afar.

224
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:47,900
And then the horse-drawn trolleys

225
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:52,100
would bring the skaters to the park.

226
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:54,180
In anticipation of the metropolis,

227
00:13:54,280 --> 00:13:57,640
Manhattan would one day become, Olmstead and Vaux

228
00:13:57,740 --> 00:14:01,540
engineered access from the east side to the west.

229
00:14:01,541 --> 00:14:07,780
Here, Olmstead and Vox conceived of grade separation of traffic.

230
00:14:07,781 --> 00:14:11,180
Grade separation means sunken transverse roads

231
00:14:11,181 --> 00:14:17,180
below the level of the park, allowing this busy city traffic

232
00:14:17,181 --> 00:14:21,900
to go through without impeding the experience of the park

233
00:14:21,901 --> 00:14:25,500
by the visitors because the carriage drives were carried

234
00:14:25,501 --> 00:14:30,200
over these transverse roads and pathways were carried over them.

235
00:14:30,201 --> 00:14:33,440
And the roads themselves were artfully screened

236
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:35,240
by the way the land was sculpted.

237
00:14:35,340 --> 00:14:39,880
So when you're in the park, you're hardly ever aware

238
00:14:39,980 --> 00:14:42,280
that there is city traffic.

239
00:14:42,380 --> 00:14:44,740
Olmstead's grade separation of traffic

240
00:14:44,840 --> 00:14:48,160
is the model for freeway underpasses and overpasses

241
00:14:48,260 --> 00:14:49,360
today.

242
00:14:49,460 --> 00:14:53,260
Within the park, they employ this principle of grade

243
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:57,620
separation again and carry the carriage drives

244
00:14:57,621 --> 00:15:02,460
over the pedestrian paths, and the pedestrian paths go under

245
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:05,920
through beautiful stone arches, most of them

246
00:15:06,020 --> 00:15:08,160
designed by Calvert Vox.

247
00:15:08,260 --> 00:15:12,100
And the people that are strolling in the park

248
00:15:12,200 --> 00:15:14,440
are carried over the bridal trail

249
00:15:14,540 --> 00:15:18,840
on beautiful ornamental cast iron bridges.

250
00:15:18,940 --> 00:15:22,580
Victorian children didn't travel the pedestrian paths.

251
00:15:22,680 --> 00:15:24,740
They had their own private area.

252
00:15:24,741 --> 00:15:29,740
It was a natural outcropping of rock 435 million years old,

253
00:15:29,840 --> 00:15:32,480
known as the Kinderberg.

254
00:15:32,580 --> 00:15:35,780
Atop the rock sat a rustic shelter built of tree limbs

255
00:15:35,880 --> 00:15:37,480
and opened to the sky.

256
00:15:37,580 --> 00:15:42,260
Nearby was a Victorian structure called the Dairy.

257
00:15:42,360 --> 00:15:43,760
You could go to the dairy.

258
00:15:43,860 --> 00:15:47,600
You could perhaps rent a toy, get a glass of milk.

259
00:15:47,700 --> 00:15:48,900
There never was a cow.

260
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,860
The milk was actually brought in from the farm.

261
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:54,200
But children could have refreshment.

262
00:15:54,201 --> 00:16:00,400
and mothers could sit and relax in this very gentle,

263
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:02,500
lovely area.

264
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:06,520
The children's carousel, built in 1871,

265
00:16:06,620 --> 00:16:08,880
was powered by mules that pulled the ride

266
00:16:08,980 --> 00:16:11,380
beneath the carousel.

267
00:16:11,480 --> 00:16:16,620
In 1916, the mules were replaced by electricity.

268
00:16:16,720 --> 00:16:18,660
For the socially prominent of New York,

269
00:16:18,760 --> 00:16:21,100
Olmstead built drives for the leisurely parade

270
00:16:21,200 --> 00:16:23,700
of fine carriages, entering the park

271
00:16:23,701 --> 00:16:26,440
each afternoon at 59th and 5th Avenues,

272
00:16:26,540 --> 00:16:30,180
the Astors rode alongside more infamous citizens,

273
00:16:30,280 --> 00:16:34,980
such as Josie Woods, the madam of New York's exclusive bordello.

274
00:16:35,080 --> 00:16:37,340
All there to see and be seen.

275
00:16:37,440 --> 00:16:40,380
For parading on foot, there was a formal promenade

276
00:16:40,480 --> 00:16:42,960
with one of the largest stands of American elms

277
00:16:43,060 --> 00:16:44,020
in the country.

278
00:16:44,120 --> 00:16:46,720
In fact, inspired by trips to England,

279
00:16:46,820 --> 00:16:49,400
Olmstead envisioned trees would line the park

280
00:16:49,500 --> 00:16:52,300
and obscure the city.

281
00:16:52,301 --> 00:16:55,060
But the invention of the elevator by Elijah Graves

282
00:16:55,160 --> 00:16:56,740
Otis ruined his plan.

283
00:16:56,840 --> 00:16:59,600
High-rise apartments sprung up all around the park,

284
00:16:59,700 --> 00:17:03,040
outreaching the trees.

285
00:17:03,140 --> 00:17:06,980
The mall in Central Park, which begins with the literary walk

286
00:17:07,080 --> 00:17:13,760
at 65th Street and goes up to 72nd Street, where the axis

287
00:17:13,860 --> 00:17:18,860
then drops below 72nd in one of those grade separated

288
00:17:18,861 --> 00:17:22,760
underpasses through the beautiful triple arches

289
00:17:22,860 --> 00:17:24,700
of the Bethesda Terrace.

290
00:17:24,800 --> 00:17:26,940
Whose fountain is the only sculpture

291
00:17:27,040 --> 00:17:29,440
included in the original Greensward design.

292
00:17:32,440 --> 00:17:37,140
You come out onto the lovely, lovely terrace

293
00:17:37,240 --> 00:17:39,980
with its great ornamental stonework,

294
00:17:40,080 --> 00:17:43,420
some of the finest 19th century stonework.

295
00:17:43,520 --> 00:17:46,620
And then all that formality dissolves

296
00:17:46,621 --> 00:17:50,120
into the surrounding scenery of the lake and the ramble,

297
00:17:50,220 --> 00:17:51,320
the yacht.

298
00:17:51,420 --> 00:17:53,860
In this, the age of Wordsworth,

299
00:17:53,960 --> 00:17:57,160
Victorians were passionately interested in botany,

300
00:17:57,260 --> 00:18:01,000
in trees, in rural impressions, and picturesque scenery.

301
00:18:01,100 --> 00:18:05,980
And Central Park was arranged as a series of those scenes.

302
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:11,220
By 1873, 16 years and $14 million since they'd begun,

303
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:13,940
Central Park was complete.

304
00:18:13,941 --> 00:18:17,640
Walking through the wilderness of the 37 hilly wooded acres

305
00:18:17,740 --> 00:18:19,720
of what is still called the Ramble,

306
00:18:19,820 --> 00:18:22,620
it was inconceivable that in the century to come,

307
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:25,420
Central Park, like the city that surrounded it,

308
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:27,160
would fall into disrepair.

309
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:33,560
Frederick Law Olmsted, the first landscape architect,

310
00:18:33,660 --> 00:18:36,260
also designed dozens of other public areas,

311
00:18:36,360 --> 00:18:39,840
including Brooklyn's Prospect Park and New York City's

312
00:18:39,940 --> 00:18:41,760
Riverside Drive Park.

313
00:18:41,761 --> 00:18:44,760
City Parks will continue on Modern Marvels.

314
00:18:48,860 --> 00:18:52,200
We now return to City Parks on Modern Marvels.

315
00:18:55,100 --> 00:18:58,180
After Frederick Law Olmsted's death in 1903,

316
00:18:58,280 --> 00:19:00,980
Central Park began to change.

317
00:19:01,080 --> 00:19:04,620
In the 1920s, the casino, once the Ladies' Pavilion,

318
00:19:04,720 --> 00:19:06,380
became a nightclub.

319
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:09,120
Park police were pressed to curb vandalism,

320
00:19:09,121 --> 00:19:13,300
And the Great Depression brought in hordes of homeless.

321
00:19:13,400 --> 00:19:16,300
In the passing of time, the Croton Reservoir

322
00:19:16,400 --> 00:19:19,900
proved too small to serve the water needs of the city.

323
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:21,400
So it was drained.

324
00:19:21,500 --> 00:19:26,340
And in the 1930s, there was temporary use by squatters

325
00:19:26,440 --> 00:19:28,180
during the Depression.

326
00:19:28,280 --> 00:19:30,680
In the 1950s, concerned citizens

327
00:19:30,780 --> 00:19:33,880
squashed the misappropriation of parkland

328
00:19:33,980 --> 00:19:37,440
for a proposed parking lot for Tavern on the Green.

329
00:19:37,441 --> 00:19:43,880
In the 1960s, Central Park was home to hippies and activists.

330
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:49,480
We had love-ins and be-ins and happenings and protest marches.

331
00:19:49,580 --> 00:19:52,120
And this was the 60s.

332
00:19:52,220 --> 00:19:54,560
And the 60s were not kind to the park

333
00:19:54,660 --> 00:19:57,060
because the poor old park department,

334
00:19:57,160 --> 00:19:59,660
they just threw up their hands and said, you know,

335
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:00,760
we don't know what to do.

336
00:20:03,700 --> 00:20:07,200
There was graffiti in the park, and there was vandalism

337
00:20:07,201 --> 00:20:10,500
of some of the statues and monuments.

338
00:20:10,600 --> 00:20:13,200
A lot of the playing fields were dust bowls.

339
00:20:15,840 --> 00:20:18,840
Drugs were being sold openly in the park.

340
00:20:18,940 --> 00:20:21,140
It was not a safe place to go.

341
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:22,840
It was a no man's land.

342
00:20:27,060 --> 00:20:28,960
Because to millions of New Yorkers,

343
00:20:29,060 --> 00:20:31,560
Central Park is New York City.

344
00:20:31,660 --> 00:20:34,700
In an unprecedented move, a public-private partnership

345
00:20:34,701 --> 00:20:37,940
formed in 1980 to rescue Central Park.

346
00:20:38,040 --> 00:20:42,000
The Central Park Conservancy was founded by Betsy Rogers.

347
00:20:42,100 --> 00:20:47,240
We raised $150 million from the citizens of New York.

348
00:20:47,340 --> 00:20:49,940
And of course, if you were to ask my successor

349
00:20:50,040 --> 00:20:54,520
to add on what's been raised since then,

350
00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:59,560
I think we're probably just under $200 million now

351
00:20:59,660 --> 00:21:01,860
from private citizens.

352
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:03,700
Returning to Vox and Olmstead's

353
00:21:03,701 --> 00:21:07,940
original design for Central Park that they'd named Greensward,

354
00:21:08,040 --> 00:21:10,440
the Conservancy came up with a master plan

355
00:21:10,540 --> 00:21:12,640
for Central Park's renovation.

356
00:21:12,740 --> 00:21:15,180
It tackled the structures as well as the landscapes.

357
00:21:17,920 --> 00:21:20,520
Turtle Pond at the foot of Belvedere Castle

358
00:21:20,620 --> 00:21:24,580
is once again a haven for nature.

359
00:21:24,680 --> 00:21:27,920
The stone 19th century Belvedere Castle has a facelift

360
00:21:28,020 --> 00:21:31,020
and is a natural observatory for migratory birds

361
00:21:31,120 --> 00:21:32,900
on the Atlantic Flyway.

362
00:21:32,901 --> 00:21:35,400
But the Conservancy's largest reclamation

363
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:40,800
is the 55-acre lush green Great Lawn, in Victorian times,

364
00:21:40,900 --> 00:21:43,440
the site of the Croton Reservoir.

365
00:21:43,540 --> 00:21:48,120
The decision was made to fill the old reservoir

366
00:21:48,220 --> 00:21:52,480
with the rubble that was left over from building

367
00:21:52,580 --> 00:21:54,720
Rockefeller Center.

368
00:21:54,820 --> 00:21:57,060
Dirt was thrown on top of rubble.

369
00:21:57,160 --> 00:22:01,560
It was seeded over and turned into a 55-acre Great Lawn,

370
00:22:01,561 --> 00:22:04,200
But it had no irrigation system, and over time,

371
00:22:04,300 --> 00:22:06,900
turned into a dust bowl.

372
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:08,500
It was a menace.

373
00:22:08,600 --> 00:22:11,740
And it was an eyesore.

374
00:22:11,840 --> 00:22:16,180
And in a way, it was a metaphor for how things had deteriorated

375
00:22:16,280 --> 00:22:20,140
in the park in general, and also how far things had deteriorated

376
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:21,740
in New York.

377
00:22:21,840 --> 00:22:23,920
Located at the center of Central Park,

378
00:22:24,020 --> 00:22:27,180
the Great Lawn became a symbol of the revitalization

379
00:22:27,280 --> 00:22:28,780
of New York City.

380
00:22:28,781 --> 00:22:34,120
Its restoration cost $18.2 million.

381
00:22:34,220 --> 00:22:36,180
We went to Cornell, worked with them,

382
00:22:36,280 --> 00:22:40,220
and had special soil engineered that has a lot of sand in it

383
00:22:40,320 --> 00:22:44,220
so that it wicks the water down through the grass

384
00:22:44,320 --> 00:22:46,640
whenever it rains.

385
00:22:46,740 --> 00:22:49,940
The lawn, a special hybrid of Kentucky bluegrass,

386
00:22:50,040 --> 00:22:52,640
is now irrigated by a new drainage system that

387
00:22:52,740 --> 00:22:54,780
crisscrosses the Great Lawn.

388
00:22:54,880 --> 00:22:57,780
New Yorker's reception to their new front yard in October

389
00:22:57,781 --> 00:23:01,280
of 1997 was overwhelming.

390
00:23:01,380 --> 00:23:04,960
When the gates were thrown open, children and families

391
00:23:05,060 --> 00:23:05,920
came running through.

392
00:23:06,020 --> 00:23:07,760
Balloons were released.

393
00:23:07,860 --> 00:23:10,460
Everyone took off their shoes, running barefoot

394
00:23:10,560 --> 00:23:13,900
across the Great Lawn in the heart of New York,

395
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:15,660
in the heart of Central Park.

396
00:23:15,760 --> 00:23:19,460
And if that wasn't a great symbol for the recovery

397
00:23:19,560 --> 00:23:21,940
of the city and for the recovery of the park,

398
00:23:22,040 --> 00:23:24,100
then I don't know what was.

399
00:23:24,200 --> 00:23:26,400
More than half the park's landscapes

400
00:23:26,401 --> 00:23:29,440
and historic structures have been restored.

401
00:23:29,540 --> 00:23:32,140
I particularly rejoice nowadays

402
00:23:32,240 --> 00:23:35,400
because in that newly restored area,

403
00:23:35,500 --> 00:23:38,840
you see the amateur painters have come back,

404
00:23:38,940 --> 00:23:40,540
the landscape painters.

405
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:45,160
And that's a sure sign that things are looking good.

406
00:23:45,260 --> 00:23:47,420
The canvas that is Central Park

407
00:23:47,520 --> 00:23:50,560
changed to meet the needs of contemporary New York

408
00:23:50,660 --> 00:23:53,560
while still retaining Olmsted's ideal.

409
00:23:53,561 --> 00:23:58,560
He wanted to screen out all suggestions of the buildings

410
00:23:58,660 --> 00:23:59,560
around the park.

411
00:23:59,660 --> 00:24:03,500
The park was, above all, a rural retreat.

412
00:24:03,600 --> 00:24:06,800
It was representative of the values

413
00:24:06,900 --> 00:24:13,520
that Thomas Jefferson held dear, the values of a society

414
00:24:13,620 --> 00:24:18,620
of farmers, of people that would find their highest inspiration

415
00:24:18,720 --> 00:24:23,120
in nature, in rural values.

416
00:24:23,121 --> 00:24:25,620
democratic values that still resonate

417
00:24:25,720 --> 00:24:29,460
within America's first public park.

418
00:24:29,560 --> 00:24:33,660
Coming up, a Western park as wild as it is large.

419
00:24:36,600 --> 00:24:38,740
Near the Dakota building where he died,

420
00:24:38,840 --> 00:24:41,400
yet inside Central Park is a 2 and 1

421
00:24:41,500 --> 00:24:44,480
acre site officially named Strawberry Fields,

422
00:24:44,580 --> 00:24:47,280
dedicated to the memory of John Lennon.

423
00:24:47,380 --> 00:24:48,780
Modern Marvels will continue.

424
00:24:53,120 --> 00:24:58,560
We now return to City Parks on Modern Marvels.

425
00:24:58,660 --> 00:25:02,560
A flamboyant Welsh immigrant self-titled Colonel Griffith

426
00:25:02,660 --> 00:25:06,600
Jenkins Griffith saw the tax advantage in giving Los Angeles

427
00:25:06,700 --> 00:25:12,780
over 4,000 acres of hilly chaparral land in 1896.

428
00:25:12,880 --> 00:25:17,640
He stipulated it be made into a public park named after him.

429
00:25:17,740 --> 00:25:21,640
Colonel Griffith was a lot of things.

430
00:25:21,641 --> 00:25:24,980
He was a remarkable man.

431
00:25:25,080 --> 00:25:26,580
He was energetic.

432
00:25:26,680 --> 00:25:28,280
He was visionary.

433
00:25:28,380 --> 00:25:32,980
He was also incredibly pompous, according to some people.

434
00:25:33,080 --> 00:25:36,880
And at times, folks wondered about his sanity.

435
00:25:36,980 --> 00:25:40,900
This hilly, craggy land had one obvious advantage.

436
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,300
It fronted the Los Angeles River,

437
00:25:43,400 --> 00:25:46,740
and river rights were exactly what Los Angeles needed,

438
00:25:46,840 --> 00:25:49,240
because running water can be generated

439
00:25:49,241 --> 00:25:54,080
generated into electrical power, and electricity meant growth.

440
00:25:54,180 --> 00:25:58,080
You've got Los Angeles, turn of the century.

441
00:25:58,180 --> 00:26:01,980
You have a little city with huge ambition.

442
00:26:02,080 --> 00:26:05,580
The park started a mile outside the edge of town,

443
00:26:05,680 --> 00:26:08,000
then on the edge of nowhere.

444
00:26:08,100 --> 00:26:12,400
Even so, Angelenos, all 110,000 of them,

445
00:26:12,500 --> 00:26:18,040
bragged their 4,000 acres made Central Park look like a backyard.

446
00:26:18,041 --> 00:26:20,780
Griffith boasted his would be a riverfront park

447
00:26:20,880 --> 00:26:23,780
for what he condescendingly called the plain people.

448
00:26:26,880 --> 00:26:29,920
He could envision the day when the city would grow

449
00:26:30,020 --> 00:26:31,980
all around the park.

450
00:26:32,080 --> 00:26:34,160
So in fact, he was one of the earliest

451
00:26:34,260 --> 00:26:36,800
to even figure out that the San Fernando Valley would become

452
00:26:36,900 --> 00:26:40,500
this enormous suburb within a city.

453
00:26:40,600 --> 00:26:43,140
Griffith Park remained rustic and untouched

454
00:26:43,240 --> 00:26:46,240
for its first few years because the city didn't have the budget

455
00:26:46,241 --> 00:26:47,500
to do much with it.

456
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:50,640
It's not like it is today, where when you go to Griffith

457
00:26:50,740 --> 00:26:57,480
Park and you see this sea of urban-ness with this oasis

458
00:26:57,580 --> 00:26:59,180
in the middle that is Griffith Park.

459
00:26:59,280 --> 00:27:02,860
Today, it is easy to see the preciousness

460
00:27:02,960 --> 00:27:05,300
and the remarkableness of this gift.

461
00:27:05,400 --> 00:27:10,360
But back then, it was just more open space in a small city

462
00:27:10,460 --> 00:27:14,740
that was surrounded by an incredible amount of open space.

463
00:27:14,741 --> 00:27:18,840
On a tour of the neglected park in 1902, Griffith J. Griffith

464
00:27:18,940 --> 00:27:22,140
was infuriated that people had cut down trees,

465
00:27:22,240 --> 00:27:24,980
plundering the park for profit.

466
00:27:25,080 --> 00:27:28,560
Griffith lobbied the city to guard the park, protect its

467
00:27:28,660 --> 00:27:31,720
animals, and keep its forest intact.

468
00:27:31,820 --> 00:27:34,760
And the Park Department was either

469
00:27:34,860 --> 00:27:37,400
unable to do anything about this,

470
00:27:37,500 --> 00:27:41,100
or they weren't motivated to do something about it.

471
00:27:41,200 --> 00:27:44,340
In February of 1903, things partially

472
00:27:44,341 --> 00:27:48,380
turned around for the park and Colonel Griffith.

473
00:27:48,480 --> 00:27:50,140
Named to the board of park commissioners,

474
00:27:50,240 --> 00:27:52,120
Griffith began to implement a plan

475
00:27:52,220 --> 00:27:54,220
for building roads and trails.

476
00:27:54,320 --> 00:27:58,820
The park was pretty inaccessible

477
00:27:58,920 --> 00:28:01,460
for the first few years of its history.

478
00:28:01,560 --> 00:28:07,700
To get to really the really remarkable areas of the park,

479
00:28:07,800 --> 00:28:10,160
you pretty much had to hike in.

480
00:28:10,161 --> 00:28:14,600
September 1903, the colonel has been acting strangely.

481
00:28:14,601 --> 00:28:17,000
I mean, even for him, he's been acting strangely.

482
00:28:17,001 --> 00:28:19,840
He is beginning to have this fixation

483
00:28:19,841 --> 00:28:23,000
that someone is trying to poison him.

484
00:28:23,001 --> 00:28:27,080
And his wife, his dutiful wife, Tina,

485
00:28:27,081 --> 00:28:31,720
suggests that she, he, and their son Van

486
00:28:31,721 --> 00:28:36,680
go to the Arcadia Hotel on the Palisades in Santa Monica

487
00:28:36,681 --> 00:28:37,880
for a vacation.

488
00:28:37,881 --> 00:28:40,160
Maybe he just needs a rest.

489
00:28:40,161 --> 00:28:42,420
On that vacation, Colonel Griffith

490
00:28:42,520 --> 00:28:44,500
entered their suite and told his wife Tina

491
00:28:44,600 --> 00:28:46,700
to get on her knees.

492
00:28:46,800 --> 00:28:48,900
Then he pointed a gun at her head and accused her

493
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:52,900
of being the one that was trying to poison him.

494
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:57,620
At some point during this horrific scene, he shoots.

495
00:28:57,720 --> 00:29:02,820
She's able to see it coming, and she jerks her head.

496
00:29:02,920 --> 00:29:07,860
And in jerking her head, the bullet grazes her,

497
00:29:07,861 --> 00:29:11,100
although it tears out her eye.

498
00:29:11,200 --> 00:29:13,500
Tina Griffith lived.

499
00:29:13,600 --> 00:29:16,600
After a notorious trial, the colonel was sentenced

500
00:29:16,700 --> 00:29:20,340
to two years in San Quentin prison.

501
00:29:20,440 --> 00:29:23,640
People held one of two opinions about Colonel Griffith

502
00:29:23,740 --> 00:29:25,980
when he came out of prison.

503
00:29:26,080 --> 00:29:30,280
One opinion was that he had tried to murder his wife.

504
00:29:30,380 --> 00:29:32,860
The other opinion was that he was crazy.

505
00:29:32,960 --> 00:29:35,980
Either way that you looked at it, his reputation

506
00:29:35,981 --> 00:29:39,780
his reputation was just shattered.

507
00:29:39,880 --> 00:29:42,480
This scandal so disgusted Angelino's

508
00:29:42,580 --> 00:29:46,860
that in 1905, homeowners living in an area called Griffith Peak

509
00:29:46,960 --> 00:29:49,260
renamed it Mount Hollywood.

510
00:29:49,360 --> 00:29:51,800
Work on the public park bearing Griffith's name

511
00:29:51,900 --> 00:29:54,840
came to a standstill.

512
00:29:54,940 --> 00:29:58,000
In the years after he's released from prison,

513
00:29:58,100 --> 00:30:02,040
he decides that he wants to do more things for the city

514
00:30:02,140 --> 00:30:03,600
and more things for the park.

515
00:30:03,601 --> 00:30:07,080
In fact, at one point, he bragged that he would give a million

516
00:30:07,180 --> 00:30:09,500
dollars to the city by the time it reached a million

517
00:30:09,600 --> 00:30:11,080
in population.

518
00:30:11,180 --> 00:30:15,080
Clearly, it was an effort to make up for his misdeeds.

519
00:30:15,180 --> 00:30:18,540
But Griffith also had a personal fantasy,

520
00:30:18,640 --> 00:30:22,380
to build an observatory and a theater in his park.

521
00:30:22,480 --> 00:30:25,260
Again, the Colonel's stained reputation

522
00:30:25,360 --> 00:30:28,020
bogged down these projects.

523
00:30:28,120 --> 00:30:30,520
With Griffith Park's formal annexation

524
00:30:30,521 --> 00:30:33,260
to the city of Los Angeles in 1910,

525
00:30:33,360 --> 00:30:36,400
roads were laid and visitors could finally access

526
00:30:36,500 --> 00:30:39,600
the sprawling jumble of topography.

527
00:30:39,700 --> 00:30:43,460
By 1913, there was even a small zoo,

528
00:30:43,560 --> 00:30:46,100
but hardly the great zoo the colonel said

529
00:30:46,200 --> 00:30:49,740
the great city deserved.

530
00:30:49,840 --> 00:30:51,880
At the very beginning, the large animals

531
00:30:51,980 --> 00:30:54,140
weren't put in cages.

532
00:30:54,240 --> 00:30:55,820
They were stockaded.

533
00:30:55,920 --> 00:30:58,440
Wire was wrapped around groups of trees,

534
00:30:58,441 --> 00:31:02,040
and the animals were just sort of pinned in in that way.

535
00:31:02,140 --> 00:31:03,940
Now, that kind of worked for the livestock,

536
00:31:04,040 --> 00:31:07,120
but fairly early on, it was realized that that wouldn't

537
00:31:07,220 --> 00:31:09,220
work for the big cats.

538
00:31:09,320 --> 00:31:13,720
It took Griffith's death at age 67 in 1919

539
00:31:13,820 --> 00:31:16,920
before Los Angeles would accept one more dollar

540
00:31:17,020 --> 00:31:19,560
from the colonel.

541
00:31:19,660 --> 00:31:22,740
On the one hand, you don't have the negative baggage

542
00:31:22,840 --> 00:31:27,040
of the Griffith name, at least not with him walking around.

543
00:31:27,041 --> 00:31:30,640
And secondly, you don't have to deal with his trying

544
00:31:30,740 --> 00:31:32,880
to run everything.

545
00:31:32,980 --> 00:31:37,280
11 years after the colonel's death in 1919,

546
00:31:37,380 --> 00:31:40,380
Los Angeles spent his money on the Greek theater.

547
00:31:40,480 --> 00:31:44,500
It opened September 25 of 1930.

548
00:31:44,600 --> 00:31:47,600
Opening night was very splashy.

549
00:31:47,700 --> 00:31:50,200
It was full house.

550
00:31:50,300 --> 00:31:52,640
But then very shortly after that, the city

551
00:31:52,740 --> 00:31:57,040
begins to run out of ideas as to what to put there.

552
00:31:57,041 --> 00:31:59,340
The Greek theater became a major venue

553
00:31:59,440 --> 00:32:01,980
for the Works Progress Administration theater

554
00:32:02,080 --> 00:32:05,780
productions in the 30s, productions that both employed

555
00:32:05,880 --> 00:32:08,520
and starred anyone who was out of work.

556
00:32:08,620 --> 00:32:10,780
For example, somebody had found out

557
00:32:10,880 --> 00:32:14,520
there was an unusual concentration of out-of-work

558
00:32:14,620 --> 00:32:16,620
clowns in the Los Angeles area.

559
00:32:16,720 --> 00:32:20,700
And so children's programming was done quite a bit at the Greek.

560
00:32:20,800 --> 00:32:23,560
In contrast to the Greek theater's unique,

561
00:32:23,660 --> 00:32:26,900
if not shaky start, the Griffith Observe

562
00:32:26,901 --> 00:32:29,540
was an unqualified hit.

563
00:32:29,640 --> 00:32:33,300
Roosevelt's New Deal made the Art Deco Observatory's opening

564
00:32:33,400 --> 00:32:37,040
in 1935 a reality.

565
00:32:37,140 --> 00:32:40,940
I think that one of the things that the Great Depression

566
00:32:41,040 --> 00:32:43,660
perhaps made possible for the observatory

567
00:32:43,760 --> 00:32:48,560
were the pure copper domes, pure copper sheets.

568
00:32:48,660 --> 00:32:53,960
They made the observatory look like it had copper pennies

569
00:32:54,060 --> 00:32:56,900
atop it when it opened.

570
00:32:56,901 --> 00:32:59,060
Out front, federally funded artists

571
00:32:59,160 --> 00:33:02,900
created a 40-foot tall star-shaped poured concrete

572
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:04,540
opelisk.

573
00:33:04,640 --> 00:33:07,780
All the astronomers appear to be done in the same style,

574
00:33:07,880 --> 00:33:11,580
but they were actually done by six different sculptors.

575
00:33:11,680 --> 00:33:13,680
One of the artists, George Stanley,

576
00:33:13,780 --> 00:33:17,820
also created Oscar for the Academy of Arts and Sciences.

577
00:33:17,920 --> 00:33:22,320
Any one of these statues could have been Oscar's uncle.

578
00:33:22,420 --> 00:33:24,520
The murals inside the central rotunda

579
00:33:24,521 --> 00:33:27,320
were painted by artist, novelist, and film producer

580
00:33:27,420 --> 00:33:28,920
Hugo Ballen.

581
00:33:29,020 --> 00:33:32,360
All this would have been cake frosting to the late Colonel.

582
00:33:32,460 --> 00:33:35,800
For him, the observatory was the telescope.

583
00:33:35,900 --> 00:33:39,140
When the Colonel envisioned the observatory,

584
00:33:39,240 --> 00:33:42,300
he really thought that public access to a great telescope

585
00:33:42,400 --> 00:33:45,000
would be a good thing for people to have.

586
00:33:45,100 --> 00:33:50,880
He knew that it wouldn't be as powerful in terms of research

587
00:33:50,980 --> 00:33:54,080
as a telescope would be in a more remote area where the skies

588
00:33:54,081 --> 00:33:55,540
were perfectly dark.

589
00:33:55,640 --> 00:33:57,580
But his goal was to bring science,

590
00:33:57,680 --> 00:33:59,680
to bring heavens to the people.

591
00:33:59,780 --> 00:34:02,860
Griffith believed that once you could see the heavens,

592
00:34:02,960 --> 00:34:06,000
you could learn your place in the universe.

593
00:34:06,100 --> 00:34:08,800
Griffith's plane people observe the world

594
00:34:08,900 --> 00:34:11,940
through a 12-inch ice refracting telescope

595
00:34:12,040 --> 00:34:13,840
installed on the roof.

596
00:34:13,940 --> 00:34:17,740
In 1937, the observatory's first great celestial event,

597
00:34:17,840 --> 00:34:23,740
Finnsler's Comet, was viewed by a record 12,000 people.

598
00:34:23,741 --> 00:34:27,640
Another 1930s addition to the park was the merry-go-round.

599
00:34:27,740 --> 00:34:31,240
Walt Disney so admired the Spillman Carousel of Dancing

600
00:34:31,340 --> 00:34:35,120
Horses that he insisted on a copy for Disneyland's opening

601
00:34:35,220 --> 00:34:36,120
in 1955.

602
00:34:41,460 --> 00:34:44,860
The children of Los Angeles have Charlie Atkins to thank

603
00:34:44,960 --> 00:34:47,400
for Griffith Park's travel town.

604
00:34:47,500 --> 00:34:50,440
In the 1950s, Atkins, a private citizen,

605
00:34:50,540 --> 00:34:52,540
began acquiring the steam locomotives

606
00:34:52,541 --> 00:34:55,340
that were being retired across the country.

607
00:34:55,440 --> 00:34:57,940
Atkins hit upon the idea of asking the locomotives

608
00:34:58,040 --> 00:35:00,780
to be donated to the park for a tax deduction

609
00:35:00,880 --> 00:35:04,080
instead of selling them off for scrap.

610
00:35:04,180 --> 00:35:06,220
Today, Griffith Park also contains

611
00:35:06,320 --> 00:35:08,820
the Autry Museum of Western Heritage

612
00:35:08,920 --> 00:35:11,760
and the Equestrian Center, as well as the world-class Los

613
00:35:11,860 --> 00:35:17,360
Angeles Zoo, now 113 acres.

614
00:35:17,460 --> 00:35:20,700
Griffith Park has always been seen as a place for people

615
00:35:20,800 --> 00:35:21,660
of modest means.

616
00:35:21,661 --> 00:35:25,260
during the Great Depression, it was a place for people to go

617
00:35:25,360 --> 00:35:27,160
who had no money.

618
00:35:27,260 --> 00:35:30,160
There were plenty of things that you could do with no money.

619
00:35:30,260 --> 00:35:34,100
By the 1950s, there was a columnist

620
00:35:34,200 --> 00:35:36,400
for the old Los Angeles Daily News.

621
00:35:36,500 --> 00:35:39,080
And he said that Griffith Park was not a place

622
00:35:39,180 --> 00:35:41,840
for lacquered blondes and Cadillacs.

623
00:35:41,940 --> 00:35:46,320
It was a place for people with 46 Pontiacs with fender dents.

624
00:35:46,420 --> 00:35:50,220
Griffith Park is still an affordable escape for families.

625
00:35:50,221 --> 00:35:53,120
Hikers make use of 53 miles of trails,

626
00:35:53,220 --> 00:35:56,020
43 of which you can take by foot or horseback.

627
00:35:56,120 --> 00:35:59,600
Just 20% of Griffith parks over 4,000 acres

628
00:35:59,700 --> 00:36:01,800
have been landscaped or built upon.

629
00:36:01,900 --> 00:36:07,140
The rest is as Griffith wanted it, rustic and immense.

630
00:36:07,240 --> 00:36:11,000
Next, a green oasis built entirely on sand.

631
00:36:13,840 --> 00:36:16,040
The Griffith Observatory was the location

632
00:36:16,140 --> 00:36:18,740
for the famous knife fight in Rebel Without a Cause,

633
00:36:18,741 --> 00:36:21,540
starring James Dean and Natalie Wood.

634
00:36:21,640 --> 00:36:23,440
Modern Marvels will return.

635
00:36:28,580 --> 00:36:30,680
We now return to Modern Marvels.

636
00:36:34,320 --> 00:36:37,360
Before the discovery of gold, San Francisco

637
00:36:37,460 --> 00:36:41,660
was a very small trading port community with less than 1,000

638
00:36:41,760 --> 00:36:43,100
people living there.

639
00:36:43,200 --> 00:36:47,160
After 1848, it ballooned into a major city with thousands

640
00:36:47,161 --> 00:36:49,160
of people arriving in ships.

641
00:36:49,260 --> 00:36:52,360
Miners blanketed the hills with makeshift clapboard

642
00:36:52,460 --> 00:36:54,660
houses and tents.

643
00:36:54,760 --> 00:36:57,100
Streets were just mires of mud.

644
00:36:57,200 --> 00:37:00,300
There were stories of mules sinking up to their bellies

645
00:37:00,400 --> 00:37:02,700
in the mud or disappearing entirely

646
00:37:02,800 --> 00:37:04,520
in the mud of the streets.

647
00:37:04,620 --> 00:37:07,520
Lands outside the city were home to vagrants,

648
00:37:07,620 --> 00:37:12,420
exiled by San Francisco's vigilante committee.

649
00:37:12,520 --> 00:37:13,720
If you could live anywhere else,

650
00:37:13,820 --> 00:37:17,160
you probably would choose to not live in the Great Sand Waste.

651
00:37:17,161 --> 00:37:22,160
Much of the over 1,000 acres was a barren waste of shifting sand

652
00:37:22,260 --> 00:37:25,640
50 feet deep on the western part of the peninsula.

653
00:37:25,740 --> 00:37:28,640
Yet in 1868, when the mayor of San Francisco

654
00:37:28,740 --> 00:37:30,880
surveyed sites available for a park,

655
00:37:30,980 --> 00:37:33,540
he settled on the great sand waste.

656
00:37:33,640 --> 00:37:36,820
The site cost over $810,000.

657
00:37:36,920 --> 00:37:38,920
This was to be Golden Gate Park.

658
00:37:39,020 --> 00:37:42,320
Even Frederick Law Olmsted, co-designer of Central Park,

659
00:37:42,420 --> 00:37:44,120
thought the location was madness.

660
00:37:47,160 --> 00:37:49,900
Fredrick Olmsted had already been to San Francisco

661
00:37:50,000 --> 00:37:53,000
and already told them that not only should you not build a park

662
00:37:53,100 --> 00:37:56,200
there, but he did not think that you could plant a tree there

663
00:37:56,300 --> 00:37:57,800
and that the tree would grow.

664
00:37:57,900 --> 00:38:00,500
William Hammond Hall was Golden Gate Park's

665
00:38:00,600 --> 00:38:02,500
first superintendent.

666
00:38:02,600 --> 00:38:06,380
In 1870, in the face of certain public humiliation,

667
00:38:06,480 --> 00:38:11,080
he courageously began to prepare the sand for planting.

668
00:38:11,180 --> 00:38:16,420
It was started off with grasses and barley and lupine,

669
00:38:16,421 --> 00:38:18,560
Whatever seeds they experimented,

670
00:38:18,660 --> 00:38:20,960
it was really a large experimentation.

671
00:38:21,060 --> 00:38:25,360
And whatever would grow is what became what they would use.

672
00:38:25,460 --> 00:38:28,960
Wrought by human hand without the benefit of nature,

673
00:38:29,060 --> 00:38:32,500
on a foundation of sand, William Hammond Hall

674
00:38:32,600 --> 00:38:37,500
designed a tract of trees, shrubs, flowers, vistas, meadows,

675
00:38:37,600 --> 00:38:39,140
lakes, and waterfalls.

676
00:38:41,940 --> 00:38:45,020
By horse-drawn carts, Hall imported enough soil

677
00:38:45,021 --> 00:38:47,660
to create a layer two to three feet thick

678
00:38:47,760 --> 00:38:50,460
to plant his great forest.

679
00:38:50,560 --> 00:38:52,960
The area was almost inaccessible.

680
00:38:56,360 --> 00:38:59,360
The issue of access to the park was one that was dear

681
00:38:59,460 --> 00:39:02,880
to William Hammond Hall because the park was designed

682
00:39:02,980 --> 00:39:04,680
for all citizens.

683
00:39:04,780 --> 00:39:08,580
It was particularly democratic with a small d

684
00:39:08,680 --> 00:39:11,380
that this should be a place for all citizens, both rich

685
00:39:11,480 --> 00:39:15,020
and poor, as was the case in Central Park in New York.

686
00:39:15,021 --> 00:39:17,020
The roadway system in Golden Gate Park

687
00:39:17,120 --> 00:39:20,920
really comes forward from Olmsteadian principles again.

688
00:39:21,020 --> 00:39:24,760
It's that meandering, curvilinear system.

689
00:39:24,860 --> 00:39:27,960
Monterey cypress trees rimmed areas of the park

690
00:39:28,060 --> 00:39:32,880
where winds off the Pacific threaten fragile plant life.

691
00:39:32,980 --> 00:39:36,680
One of Golden Gate Park's oldest and most romantic edifices

692
00:39:36,780 --> 00:39:40,780
was the conservatory, which housed tropical plants.

693
00:39:40,880 --> 00:39:44,320
Prominent San Franciscans bought the massive wooden glass

694
00:39:44,321 --> 00:39:47,720
from an estate sale and donated it to the park.

695
00:39:51,860 --> 00:39:57,160
Constructed in 1879, it covered 15,000 square feet.

696
00:39:57,260 --> 00:40:02,000
The glass was held in place by three tons of putty.

697
00:40:02,100 --> 00:40:04,900
The conservatory has been closed now for about five years

698
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:08,420
due to earthquake and storm damage.

699
00:40:08,520 --> 00:40:13,440
It's extremely expensive to repair the conservatory

700
00:40:13,441 --> 00:40:18,080
to the point that it was 100 years ago.

701
00:40:18,180 --> 00:40:21,580
A gift from the Sharon estate in the late 1800s

702
00:40:21,680 --> 00:40:25,120
made possible the construction of a children's playground.

703
00:40:25,220 --> 00:40:28,660
Senator William Sharon made his fortune in silver

704
00:40:28,760 --> 00:40:31,000
in the Nevada Comstock Lode.

705
00:40:31,100 --> 00:40:34,300
There were separate wooden slides for boys and girls

706
00:40:34,400 --> 00:40:37,500
and a wonderful merry-go-round with half the wooden horses

707
00:40:37,600 --> 00:40:43,200
equipped with side saddles for proper young ladies riding.

708
00:40:43,201 --> 00:40:47,200
Beginning in 1890, Scottish-born landscaper John McLaren

709
00:40:47,300 --> 00:40:50,980
presided over Golden Gate Park as its superintendent.

710
00:40:51,080 --> 00:40:53,380
A graduate of the Edinburgh University

711
00:40:53,480 --> 00:40:55,480
with the simple title of gardener,

712
00:40:55,580 --> 00:40:58,640
McLaren believed in always working with nature,

713
00:40:58,740 --> 00:41:00,080
never against her.

714
00:41:00,180 --> 00:41:02,160
Well, one of the things that John McLaren

715
00:41:02,260 --> 00:41:04,460
was proud to brag about was the fact

716
00:41:04,560 --> 00:41:07,460
that in Golden Gate Park, he had a plant growing

717
00:41:07,560 --> 00:41:09,960
from every country in the world.

718
00:41:09,961 --> 00:41:13,960
Tramping the Sierras with famed naturalist John Muir,

719
00:41:14,060 --> 00:41:17,200
John McLaren brought back ideas he translated

720
00:41:17,300 --> 00:41:21,140
into spectacular living images.

721
00:41:21,240 --> 00:41:25,200
I understand that the falls in Stow Lake

722
00:41:25,300 --> 00:41:29,080
were inspired by a comment made by John Muir.

723
00:41:29,180 --> 00:41:31,880
McLaren became known as the greatest introducer

724
00:41:31,980 --> 00:41:36,620
of exotics that the West has ever known.

725
00:41:36,621 --> 00:41:39,920
San Francisco sunk into a depression three years

726
00:41:40,020 --> 00:41:41,920
into McLaren's reign.

727
00:41:42,020 --> 00:41:45,260
To stimulate the city's economy in 1893,

728
00:41:45,360 --> 00:41:48,400
Chronicle newspaper publisher M.H. de Young

729
00:41:48,500 --> 00:41:51,660
moved into Golden Gate Park, enormous attractions

730
00:41:51,760 --> 00:41:55,080
from Chicago's Columbian Exposition.

731
00:41:55,180 --> 00:41:58,480
Promoters named it the California Midwinter Fair

732
00:41:58,580 --> 00:42:02,720
and charged admission for people to see the electric tower,

733
00:42:02,721 --> 00:42:07,720
or ride a camel in Cairo Street.

734
00:42:07,820 --> 00:42:12,300
They set up fairgrounds in the music concourse area

735
00:42:12,400 --> 00:42:17,200
of Golden Gate Park, built a 49ers mining camp,

736
00:42:17,300 --> 00:42:21,300
and they had a Japanese garden, which, of course, now

737
00:42:21,400 --> 00:42:24,800
is our Japanese tea garden.

738
00:42:32,720 --> 00:42:36,520
The tea garden was built by Japanese artisans employed

739
00:42:36,620 --> 00:42:40,020
by George Turner Marsh, a local merchant.

740
00:42:40,120 --> 00:42:43,700
Ladies in kimonos served tea and cookies for 10 cents.

741
00:42:43,800 --> 00:42:46,740
As part of the concession, Marsh transported visitors

742
00:42:46,840 --> 00:42:50,780
by rickshaws hauled by Germans dressed as Asians.

743
00:42:50,880 --> 00:42:53,480
The Japanese of San Francisco refused the work,

744
00:42:53,580 --> 00:42:57,120
saying it was an insult to the emperor of Japan.

745
00:42:57,220 --> 00:43:00,580
At the end of the fair, Japan-born Makoto Hagiwara

746
00:43:00,581 --> 00:43:04,760
Makoto Hagiwara moved into the tea garden and made it his home.

747
00:43:04,860 --> 00:43:08,260
At the time, his family thought he was crazy.

748
00:43:08,360 --> 00:43:12,560
To the Japanese, America was the land of the barbarians.

749
00:43:12,660 --> 00:43:15,100
Master gardener Makoto Hagiwara

750
00:43:15,200 --> 00:43:17,340
added the original design of the tea garden

751
00:43:17,440 --> 00:43:20,160
from the midwinter fair and ran a tea concession.

752
00:43:22,760 --> 00:43:26,180
For generations, the Hagiwara men presided over one

753
00:43:26,280 --> 00:43:30,580
of Golden Gate Park's most beloved sanctuaries.

754
00:43:30,581 --> 00:43:35,580
The Hagiwara family was able to maintain the tea garden

755
00:43:35,680 --> 00:43:38,480
from their own pocket, essentially, plus the whatever

756
00:43:38,580 --> 00:43:40,120
they could make from the concession,

757
00:43:40,220 --> 00:43:41,560
which was the tea house.

758
00:43:41,660 --> 00:43:44,400
So they sold tea and they sold cookies.

759
00:43:44,500 --> 00:43:46,560
And that, along with their own money,

760
00:43:46,660 --> 00:43:48,240
basically ran the tea garden.

761
00:43:48,340 --> 00:43:52,240
And their deal with John McLaren was $1 a year for rent.

762
00:43:52,340 --> 00:43:56,900
In 1942, America was at war with Japan.

763
00:43:56,901 --> 00:44:00,900
The Hagiwara family was no longer welcome in Golden Gate

764
00:44:01,000 --> 00:44:03,540
Park's Japanese tea garden.

765
00:44:03,640 --> 00:44:07,340
When the war broke out, as occurred with all Japanese

766
00:44:07,440 --> 00:44:10,220
families, they got eviction notices from wherever

767
00:44:10,320 --> 00:44:11,340
they were living.

768
00:44:11,440 --> 00:44:15,660
And naturally, we got ours from the garden.

769
00:44:15,760 --> 00:44:18,560
The family, like other Japanese families on the West Coast,

770
00:44:18,660 --> 00:44:20,820
were moved to a relocation camp.

771
00:44:20,920 --> 00:44:23,200
During that period, their house was destroyed

772
00:44:23,300 --> 00:44:25,700
and was taken away as scrap.

773
00:44:25,701 --> 00:44:27,660
and the name of the Japanese tea garden

774
00:44:27,760 --> 00:44:30,800
was changed to the Oriental Tea Garden.

775
00:44:30,900 --> 00:44:34,380
Today, the tea garden still has the same design and layout

776
00:44:34,480 --> 00:44:37,440
that Makoto put together many years ago.

777
00:44:37,540 --> 00:44:40,320
Famed sculptor Ruth Ozawa created a tribute

778
00:44:40,420 --> 00:44:43,680
to the hagiwaras that sits inside the front gates.

779
00:44:46,820 --> 00:44:51,460
By 1887, William Hammond Hall's original design plan

780
00:44:51,560 --> 00:44:55,700
for Golden Gate Park had truly been met.

781
00:44:55,701 --> 00:44:58,540
The Golden Gate Park came from sand dunes.

782
00:44:58,640 --> 00:45:00,280
It's not a natural park.

783
00:45:00,380 --> 00:45:03,800
And as the gardeners and all the maintenance personnel

784
00:45:03,900 --> 00:45:07,320
will tell you, every day it's trying to go back to sand dunes.

785
00:45:07,420 --> 00:45:10,120
Every day it becomes more difficult to maintain it

786
00:45:10,220 --> 00:45:12,920
the way you see it now.

787
00:45:13,020 --> 00:45:15,580
Particularly for people without means,

788
00:45:15,680 --> 00:45:19,520
Golden Gate Park, Central Park, Griffith Park, and countless

789
00:45:19,620 --> 00:45:23,900
others are an affordable break from an otherwise harsh

790
00:45:24,000 --> 00:45:25,700
existence.

791
00:45:25,701 --> 00:45:28,600
to take this free open space for granted.

792
00:45:28,700 --> 00:45:31,000
William Hammond Hall said, destroy a building

793
00:45:31,100 --> 00:45:32,940
and it could be rebuilt in a year.

794
00:45:33,040 --> 00:45:36,840
Destroy a city woodland park, and all the people living

795
00:45:36,940 --> 00:45:40,340
at the time will have passed away before its restoration

796
00:45:40,440 --> 00:45:41,340
can be effected.

